  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1131 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTemperatures will continue to be at or near-record territory on\nMonday as the area remains in the warm sector of a low pressure\nsystem approaching from the central Plains. The only limiting\nfactor on Monday will be mid and high level clouds streaming in\nfrom the west. However, models do clear out these clouds across\ncentral and east-central Wisconsin by the afternoon. Wind are not\nexpected to be as gusty, with winds of 10 to 20 mph expected.\n\nPrecip chances...The best chance for rain still appears to be\nMonday evening as the main surface low tracks through northern\nWisconsin coincident with the mid level shortwave tracking through\nthe region. The best chances (60-90%) look to be across northern\nWisconsin where the best moisture will reside with much lower\nchances (30-40%) across central and east-central Wisconsin as a\ndry slot tracks through the area. While the probability of\nexceeding 0.25\" of rain has risen to 50-80% across the north,\nchances are fairly meager south of highway 20 at 10-30%. Embedded\nnon-severe storms remain possible as corridor of elevated \ninstability (~600 to 800 J/kg) develops along the front, though \nany convective activity would remain elevated.\n\nNo further clarity provided for end of week system as ensemble\nclusters remain divided on synoptic pattern evolution. As it so\noften is, phasing potential remains a major source of uncertainty\nas some models show northern and southern streams phasing over\nthe Great Lakes, while some show this occurring further east. As\nit stands now, still looks on track to bring us our next\nwidespread precip chances, though this is subject to change as\nmodels hopefully get a better handle on the upper pattern.\nConsistent signal also remains for the first flakes of the season\nmixing in across the north along the back edge of the system.\n\nOutlook...More fall-like pattern sets up at the end of the week \ninto the weekend as arctic high brings below normal temperatures \nfrom the Plains to the Midwest as well as a return to dry \nconditions. 850 mb temperatures will tank to well below 0C as \nnorthwest flow ramps up, resulting in high temperatures reading in\nthe 50s for most. Frost/freeze setup looks likely from central to\neast-central Wisconsin as we approach the end of the growing \nseason.",
